Good news first, and it corrects something I told you earlier. For **future** events, almost everything is movable. The "66 events with attendees" scare from the past window doesn't carry forward: those were mostly one-off past meetings. Looking at the next 12 months:

## What won't move

**On the primary "Business" calendar (24 future series): only 1 is stuck**
- **"Flowcus User Research"** organized by `rhyd.lewis@gmail.com` (you're an invitee, not the organizer)

**On the APR secondary (15 future series): 0 stuck, all movable.**

The general rule for what can't move (and a correction to what I said earlier, I was wrong that Google can't move events at all):

- **Events someone else organizes** (you're just invited). Only the organizer can relocate them. That's the Flowcus one.
- **Special event types**: birthdays, out-of-office, focus-time, and Gmail-extracted events (flights/hotels auto-added from email).
- **Single instances of a recurring series** can't be peeled off alone; the script moves the *whole series* as a unit (fine, and it keeps future occurrences intact).
- **Going forward**, every invitation you accept plus every Zoom event still lands on primary automatically. Unavoidable, and it's why primary = Business is the right call.

Events you organize *do* move cleanly, in place, attendees preserved, no re-invites sent. That's ~38 of your 39 future series.

## Draft script (Google Apps Script)

Runs as you at script.google.com, no OAuth setup. Uses native `Events.move`. Dry-run by default so it only *reports* until you flip the switch. I've defaulted it to the **primary → Personal** direction (your case: pull genuinely-personal events off the old-personal primary), with a `SKIP_TITLES` list so business events stay put.

```javascript
/**
 * Move events YOU ORGANIZE from SOURCE to TARGET calendar.
 * Native Events.move: keeps recurring series + attendees, sends no re-invites.
 * SETUP: script.google.com > new project > Services (+) > "Google Calendar API" (identifier: Calendar) > Add.
 * Then paste, set DRY_RUN=false when the dry-run list looks right.
 */

// ---- config ----
const SOURCE_ID = 'mark@felicisholdings.com';                                              // "Business" (primary, old personal)
const TARGET_ID = 'c_b08f6f12800dbc0cc6a79596958489b7a7c09270350553f827aa264c3926800f@group.calendar.google.com'; // "Personal"
// To instead consolidate APR onto primary, swap to:
//   SOURCE_ID = 'smart-life-choices.com_t7e7rlr9j99ljpc78bt6blau4o@group.calendar.google.com'; TARGET_ID = 'mark@felicisholdings.com';

const DRY_RUN = true;                    // <-- false to actually move
const SKIP_TITLES = [                    // case-insensitive substrings to LEAVE in place (business-ish)
  'APR', 'client', 'coaching', 'course', 'workshop', 'sprint', 'standup'
];
// ----------------

function migrateEvents() {
  const now = Date.now();
  let token = null, would = 0, skipPast = 0, skipNotOrg = 0, skipTitle = 0, failed = 0;

  do {
    const resp = Calendar.Events.list(SOURCE_ID, {
      singleEvents: false,   // recurring MASTERS, not expanded instances
      showDeleted: false,
      maxResults: 250,
      pageToken: token
    });
    (resp.items || []).forEach(function (ev) {
      if (ev.status === 'cancelled') return;
      const label = (ev.summary || '(no title)') + ' @' + ((ev.start && (ev.start.dateTime || ev.start.date)) || '?');

      if (!(ev.organizer && ev.organizer.self)) { skipNotOrg++; Logger.log('SKIP not-organizer: ' + label); return; }

      const recurring = !!ev.recurrence;
      if (!recurring) {                                   // past one-offs: skip
        const endV = ev.end && (ev.end.dateTime || ev.end.date);
        if (endV && new Date(endV).getTime() < now) { skipPast++; return; }
      }

      if (SKIP_TITLES.some(function (t) { return (ev.summary || '').toLowerCase().indexOf(t.toLowerCase()) !== -1; })) {
        skipTitle++; Logger.log('SKIP title-filter: ' + label); return;
      }

      if (DRY_RUN) { would++; Logger.log('WOULD MOVE' + (recurring ? ' [series]' : '') + ': ' + label); return; }

      try {
        Calendar.Events.move(SOURCE_ID, ev.id, TARGET_ID, { sendUpdates: 'none' });
        would++; Logger.log('MOVED: ' + label);
      } catch (e) { failed++; Logger.log('FAILED: ' + label + ' -> ' + e.message); }
    });
    token = resp.nextPageToken;
  } while (token);

  Logger.log('--- ' + (DRY_RUN ? '[DRY RUN] would move ' : 'moved ') + would +
    ' | skipped: past ' + skipPast + ', not-organizer ' + skipNotOrg + ', title ' + skipTitle + ', failed ' + failed);
}
```

**How to run:**
1. script.google.com → new project → **Services (+)** → add **Google Calendar API** (identifier `Calendar`).
2. Paste, keep `DRY_RUN = true`, run `migrateEvents`, authorize when prompted.
3. **View → Logs** (or Ctrl/Cmd+Enter). Read the `WOULD MOVE` / `SKIP` list.
4. Tune `SKIP_TITLES` until the split looks right, then set `DRY_RUN = false` and run again.

Two flags before you run it:

- **Recurring series bring their whole history.** `Events.move` moves the entire series, so past occurrences travel too. Harmless, just noting it, since you said you only care about future.
- **Confirm the direction.** I defaulted to primary → Personal because your "many of these are actually business" comment only applies when splitting the old personal calendar. If you instead want to fold the APR secondary onto primary (all 15 move, no title-filtering needed), use the swap line in the config.

Want me to adjust the `SKIP_TITLES` keywords? If you tell me roughly what's genuinely personal on that calendar, I can tighten the filter so the dry-run comes out closer to correct on the first pass.
